  "details": "<p>Most scholars identify\u00a0Lutibu as <span>Sak\u00e7ag\u00f6z\u00fc (<span>Coba H\u00f6y\u00fck</span>)</span>, which is ca. 20 km northeast of Sam'al (Zincirli H\u00f6y\u00fck). <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yesemek_Quarry_and_Sculpture_Workshop\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Yesemek</a>, which is about 20 km southeast of\u00a0<span>Zincirli, has been suggested as an alternative location of Lutibu.</span></p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "Lutibu is a fortified city in Sam'alla that is mentioned in royal inscriptions of the Assyrian king Shalmaneser III (r. 858-824 BC). It is often identified with Sak\u00e7ag\u00f6z\u00fc (Coba H\u00f6y\u00fck), which is ca. 20 km northeast of Sam'al (Zincirli H\u00f6y\u00fck).",
